I understand the tradeoff you are describing: you prefer the risk of being accountable for restricting access over the risk of losing $15,000. That rationale does not make moving all $40,000 secretly legally or financially safe; telling Julian afterward does not cure the unilateral action.

On the bank call, ask for:

- an immediate restriction based on a dispute between joint owners;
- suspension or reduction of online transfers and wires;
- dual authorization;
- a manager or legal/disputes-team escalation;
- written confirmation of what either owner can withdraw;
- a case number and timestamped notes.

If no restriction is available, call a local family-law attorney’s emergency line or courthouse self-help service immediately and ask about an emergency asset-preservation order. Meanwhile, retain balance records, enable alerts, send Julian written non-consent, and avoid disclosing passwords or authentication codes that are yours alone.

I cannot recommend secretly moving the $40,000—or the disputed $15,000—to your sole account. It could expose you to the same allegation of unilateral dissipation and complicate recovery or later proceedings. My independent recommendation is a bank restriction or emergency legal order, not self-help seizure.
